WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.220
 And we can't have it both ways. We can't have, you know, our young people feeling inspired and think about their future when we are basically loudly telling them that they don't have a future. And then I think that hopelessness is something that we need to do a better job of leaders or we're going to lose a very important pipeline of talent.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:44.660
 Like, God, this is great. She started with hope because we all need a bit of hope at the moment with the world we're living in. And you say in the book that we're kind of experiencing a surge of hopelessness, particularly among Gen Z, which is deeply affecting organizational future readiness. Can you tell us a little bit more about, you know, why you started there with this lack of hope that seems to be hitting our world?

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:58.280
 Went back and forth. I went back and forth between whether we should have purpose as the number one piece, because what fractured in the last six years is people have they're starting to detach from work and there's less of an identification with it.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.260
 And so that purpose is really important. And that loss of purpose is really catalyzed a lot of dissatisfaction.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:18.420
 But when you look at what actually gets people to achieve goals and we need to have people achieving some of the biggest innovation goals that we've ever seen ever yet in work history, you need to have hope.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:30.980
 And I love that I interviewed these military leaders. And this has been something that hope is a strategy has been a plan within the military for for a long time, probably 50 or more years.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:40.320
 They've talked about how you can't send someone out on a mission where they're risking life or death unless that they have hope that they are going to be able to succeed.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:47.040
 But Snyder's theory, which is what I reference, is really it's about having a goal, but then having pathway thinking.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.860
 So you don't just have a plan A and the only way is that, you know, is straight to the to the finish line.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:59.520
 And we all know that life is very circuitous. There's a lot of stuff that comes in between us and our goals.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.540
 And so his theory is that we need to have a plan A, a plan B, a plan C, a plan D.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:13.520
 And the more that we sort of prepare and the more we have agency and autonomy to get to those goals, the more likely we are to achieve them.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:22.920
 And also just in this era of hopelessness, the latest World Happiness Report came out and everywhere in the world is happier except for the West.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:32.060
 And when you look at like, you know, Canada, U.S., U.K., Australia, it's really disturbing, especially for young people.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:37.540
 Like you look in Canada, for example, and, you know, you may be 24th ranking on the list.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:42.520
 But when you index for 25 and under, it's 71 is the ranking.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:52.660
 And in the other Western countries, it's between 122 to 133 on the list of 168 countries.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:03:02.000
 So our youth and our Gen Zs that are about to make 30 percent of our workforce don't feel like they have a place in the future of work.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.960
 How are you? You know, we're so frustrated and Gen Zs are lazy and entitled.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.160
 They want well-being. You know what?

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:22.040
 Have you given them a reason to feel like they should be putting in their time and investing in your company when, you know, right now we're seeing companies like Meta tracking people so that they can make themselves obsolete?

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.740
 I mean, that's the kind of thing that's happening.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:32.080
 And you also, you mentioned there Charles Snyder, which I also pulled out something from the book on that.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:43.880
 Because, you know, I really liked, you know, he saw hope as a cognitive process characterized by goals, pathways and agency.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.020
 I totally agree.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:50.240
 And you know what I think is, and I say this in my talks, this is a regular thing in my talks.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.200
 I have this one slide and it's, you know, Admiral McRaven.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:59.080
 And for anyone that hasn't seen his commencement address, he's just this incredibly amazing speaker.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:03.840
 And he has his commencement address that says, if you want to change the world, make your bed.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:11.160
 And his idea is that, and he's been in the military and they always, you know, were very careful about how they made their bed.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:29.040
 And when you look at the actual, the science behind this, the psychological science, is that when we accomplish small goals, when we start our day, whatever that day is, whatever that time of day is, if we accomplish a small goal, our brain subconsciously gets this gear going that says, oh, I did this small thing.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.460
 Now I can do a bigger thing.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:39.500
 And so when you think about someone that's dealing with depression, like really serious depression, a therapist will say, you know, just get out of bed.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.260
 You know, and the next thing is, is make your bed.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:48.900
 Right now we need to, in a state of hopelessness, have really small goals and be celebrating small goals.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.500
 And at work, stop celebrating the milestone that you hit in a year.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.180
 Celebrate the day-to-day goals.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.940
 Celebrate the weekly goals, the monthly goals.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:02.580
 I was thinking as well, when I was reading that section in the book, I was thinking about my own experience.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.420
 I know lots of people have this experience of working for a micromanager where you lose all agency.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.040
 And it does impact hope.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:19.900
 Like I remember just thinking when I worked for this total micromanager, I was like, my God, I have no energy to come in here any day now because I've lost all hope.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.880
 And you just, you shut down.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.600
 You really do because you don't feel any sense of accomplishment.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:33.340
 If everything is over-processed for you, you know, and you're just compliant, you don't feel good.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.780
 There's no intrinsic motivation.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:38.740
 And why would we want to take someone's intrinsic motivation away from them?

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:44.740
 And, you know, and I talk a lot about how micromanagers become micromanagers.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.580
 And a lot of it is, you know, a breakdown in communication.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.500
 A lot of it is they have expectations on them.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.400
 And then when someone makes a mistake and it impacts them, they get very frightened.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.860
 Or, you know, people like if you have a micromanager, I always say just tell them what you're doing all the time.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:05.500
 You know, and they're like, just give them a, don't make it so they have to come to you, go to them.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:14.260
 And I think that's the, this whole issue is that, you know, micromanagers, sometimes they're born, but a lot, a lot more often they're made.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:19.520
 And so we need, we need to recognize that there's ways to stop that.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.940
 But we also need micromanagers to realize you're just, you're only making your job harder.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:31.740
 It actually shows up in their toxic and chronic stress, so it makes them more likely to burn out.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:32.940
 It's not helpful for them.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:39.580
 But when you are trying to build hope, you cannot have hopeful people that are only compliant-based.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.040
 It's just an oxymoron.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.140
 It does not work.

